READ, NOT MEASURED -- graded honestly, and it is a sibling of pitlatch rather than part of it. dev_8253.c's mode-write arm is UNCONDITIONAL:

    d->mode_byte = idata;
    d->counter_select = (idata >> 6) & 3;
    d->mode[d->counter_select] = idata & 0x0e;

A real 8254 COUNTER-LATCH command does NOT disturb the programmed RW format or the counting mode -- it only latches the count. Here a latch write CLOBBERS mode_byte (destroying the RW format the guest set earlier) and ZEROES d->mode[counter], because the latch command's mode bits are 0.

SO EVEN A GUEST THAT NEVER READS THE LATCHED VALUE HAS HAD ITS COUNTER SILENTLY REPROGRAMMED. That is a distinct consequence from pitlatch's (a latch-then-read returns 0 and floods the host log): this one damages state on the WRITE alone.

Reachability inherits pitlatch's: OpenBSD's microtime() issues the latch command (_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/i386/isa/clock.c:207-209), so the clobber happens on every call. NOT MEASURED -- no probe has yet shown the downstream effect of the corrupted RW format, and the honest grade is READ until one does. The pitlatch panel was asked prong by prong whether this travels in the same round or its own.

*** THE BIGGEST THING THE WHOLE PIT CLUSTER MISSED, AND IT REFRAMES ALL FIVE ROWS AS SURFACE MANIFESTATIONS. ***  READ, from the in-tree guest source.

_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/i386/isa/clock.c:266-275, delay():
    while (n > 0) {
        tick = gettick();
        if (tick > otick) n -= limit - (tick - otick);
        else              n -= otick - tick;
        otick = tick;
    }
If gettick() returns a CONSTANT, tick == otick every iteration, n -= 0, and the loop never terminates. That holds for the shipped #439 fix (constant 0), for the refused candidate C1 (constant 0xffff), AND for the committed pre-#439 build (constant 0). delay() is called all over a PC kernel.

*** SO #439 IS GENUINELY 'NO WORSE THAN BEFORE', WHICH IS WHAT IT CLAIMS ABOUT READ VALUES -- BUT IT IS NOT 'SAFE', AND I SHOULD NOT HAVE LET THAT WORD STAND. ***  The only thing that fixes delay() is modelling a count that decrements, which is dev_8253.c's own TODO and is none of the five filed rows.

SEVERITY CEILING, stated plainly because it governs scheduling: no rig image boots any 8253 machine (regress/images.md lists luna88k/m88k, landisk/SH4, macppc/PPC). Guest-visible harm TODAY on every row in this cluster is ZERO. None of them can ever be a 'measured false pass', so under the stopping rule they are all FILE-then-schedule, never emergency work.
